when published in full that April. Ironically, the very same prosecutor, Pinard, would go on to prosecute Baudelaire's *Les Fleurs du Mal* later that same year — with less success for the defendant.\n\n### Historical Significance\nThe trial set an important precedent for literary freedom in France and across Europe. It established that artistic intent and moral consequence within a narrative could serve as legitimate defenses against obscenity charges — a debate that would echo through the 20th century in landmark cases involving D.H. Lawrence, Henry Miller, and others.\n\n---\n\n## 2. 🗺️ Plot Summary & Main Characters\n\n### Setting\nProvincial Normandy, France, roughly **1830s–1840s**. The towns of Tostes and Yonville-l'Abbaye (a fictional stand-in for Ry, Normandy) form the claustrophobic backdrop.\n\n### Plot Overview\n\n**Part One:** We are introduced first to **Charles Bovary**, a slow-witted, kind-hearted country doctor, through the eyes of his schoolmates. He marries a widow, who dies; he then marries **Emma Rouault**, the educated, romantically imaginative daughter of a wealthy farmer. Emma has been raised on convent-school novels and harbors grand fantasies of passionate love and elegant society. Almost immediately after the wedding, she finds herself profoundly disappointed — Charles is devoted but dull, and provincial life is suffocating. A ball at the Château de la Vaubyessard, where Emma briefly glimpses aristocratic splendor, deepens her longing and discontent. The couple moves to Yonville.\n\n**Part Two:** Emma gives birth to a daughter, Berthe, but motherhood brings no fulfillment. She is courted by **Léon Dupuis**, a young law clerk with literary sensibilities. Their relationship remains chaste — Léon eventually leaves for Paris — but Emma's longing intensifies. She then falls into an affair with **Rodolphe Boulanger**, a wealthy, cynical landowner who pursues her deliberately. Emma falls deeply in love and fantasizes about eloping with him; Rodolp